# XCU umask
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# umask is intrinsic (XCU 1.7). Effects on creation are observed with files
# and directories the test makes in its private directory; the mode is
# read back with ls -ld, using only the first ten characters.

assertion  XCU.umask.001
anchor     utilities/umask.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/umask.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/umask.html#STDOUT
class      required
since      1992
says       umask with an octal mask sets the mask for later creations:
           with 027 a file made by a redirection gets rw-r----- and a
           directory made by mkdir gets rwxr-x---; the call exits zero
           and writes nothing.
test       t/001-octal.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.002
anchor     utilities/umask.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/umask.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       umask with no operand writes a message that, given back as the
           operand, restores the mask; the mask in force does not change
           by asking.
test       t/002-reinput.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.003
anchor     utilities/umask.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/umask.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       umask -S writes one line u=...,g=...,o=... whose letters (from r,
           w, x) are the permissions the mask leaves clear; for mask 027
           these are rwx, rx and none.
test       t/003-symbolic-output.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.004
anchor     utilities/umask.html#STDOUT
anchor     utilities/umask.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       The line written by umask -S is accepted as the operand and
           restores the mask.
test       t/004-symbolic-reinput.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.005
anchor     utilities/umask.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       A symbolic mask u=rwx,g=rx,o= sets the mask to the complement of
           those permissions, which is 027.
test       t/005-symbolic-absolute.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.006
anchor     utilities/umask.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       In a symbolic mask the + operator clears bits in the current mask
           and the - operator sets them: from 077, g+rx gives 027; from
           000, go-w gives 022.
test       t/006-symbolic-relative.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.007
anchor     utilities/umask.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.13
class      required
since      1992
says       umask run in a subshell does not change the mask of the shell
           that started it.
test       t/007-subshell.sh

assertion  XCU.umask.008
anchor     utilities/umask.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/V3_chap01.html#1.4
class      required
since      1992
says       A mask operand that is not valid gives an exit status greater
           than zero, a diagnostic on standard error, and leaves the mask
           unchanged.
test       t/008-invalid.sh
